#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059511 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059683 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bba0ad98-6940-502a-873c-8a589a03a499" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Coterminal Angles in Degrees"}] :question/id #uuid "fae68ee3-73e6-53a1-a141-e362b8118964" :question/math-academy-id "e-3839" :question/problem "Which angles are coterminal with $240^\\circ?$\n\n$$\n600^\\circ, \\quad 300^\\circ, \\quad -480^\\circ.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the coterminal angles of a given angle $\\theta,$ we add and subtract integer multiples of $360^\\circ\\mathbin{.}$\n\nLet's start by adding multiples of $360^\\circ$ to $240^\\circ\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{240}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}={600}^{∘}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no answer choices larger than $600^\\circ,$ so we can stop here.\n\nNow, let's subtract multiples of $360^\\circ$ from $240^\\circ\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{240}^{∘}-{360}^{∘} & =-{120}^{∘} \\\\ {240}^{∘}-2\\cdot ({360}^{∘}) & =-{480}^{∘}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no answer choices smaller than $-480^\\circ,$ so we can stop here.\n\nSo, of the given angles, the coterminal angles are $600^\\circ$ and $-480^\\circ.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059512 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059684 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2d65819f-0528-527e-8d1a-20421e221ca5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Coterminal Angles in Radians"}] :question/id #uuid "5e722d3c-d592-591f-a6b6-faac38285630" :question/math-academy-id "e-3840" :question/problem "Which angle is coterminal with $\\dfrac {3 \\pi} {5}?$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4\\pi}{5}, \\quad -\\dfrac{7\\pi}{5}, \\quad -\\dfrac{11\\pi}{5}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the coterminal angles of a given angle $\\theta$ (in radians), we add and subtract integer multiples of $2\\pi.$\n\nLet's start by adding multiples of $2\\pi$ to $\\dfrac {3 \\pi} {5}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3\\pi }{5}+2\\pi =\\frac{13\\pi }{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no answer choices larger than $\\dfrac {13 \\pi} {5},$ so we can stop here.\n\nNow, let's subtract multiples of $2\\pi$ from $\\dfrac {3 \\pi} {5}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3\\pi }{5}-2\\pi  & =-\\frac{7\\pi }{5}\\,✓ \\\\ \\frac{3\\pi }{5}-2\\cdot (2\\pi ) & =-\\frac{17\\pi }{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no answer choices smaller than $-\\dfrac {17 \\pi} {5},$ so we can stop here.\n\nSo, of the given angles, the only coterminal angle is $-\\dfrac{7\\pi}{5}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059513 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059685 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "993c8fe4-dae9-5dc4-9f94-5012f905e0ee" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Correct Quadrant for Negative Angles that Contain More Than One Full Rotation"}] :question/id #uuid "1d570998-ebae-5dee-a9d7-8c439225a910" :question/math-academy-id "e-3841" :question/problem "The angle $\\theta =  -435^\\circ$ is represented in the coordinate plane in the usual way. In which quadrant does it lie?" :question/worked-solution "First, we find an angle that's coterminal with $-435^\\circ$ by *adding* integer multiples of $360^\\circ$ until we get a coterminal angle that lies in the range $[0^\\circ, 360^\\circ).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{435}^{∘}+{360}^{∘} & =-{75}^{∘} \\\\ -{75}^{∘}+{360}^{∘} & ={285}^{∘}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the angle $285^\\circ$ is coterminal with $-435^\\circ.$ Let's draw this coterminal angle in the coordinate plane.\n\n![](images/4f/4f3fc79c2fc99478c407a63352d9113ef9cbef6a93a6feb62bd284d5f443bed5.png)\n\nWe see that $285^\\circ$ lies in quadrant IV. Therefore, $-435^\\circ$ also lies in this quadrant."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059514 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059686 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0a119150-6d23-59bd-90c9-699de3a75795" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Correct Quadrant for Angles that Contain More Than One Full Rotation"}] :question/id #uuid "ede68192-5d89-5204-8c49-a1eeebfbf48c" :question/math-academy-id "e-3842" :question/problem "The angle $\\theta =  \\dfrac {8\\pi}3$ is represented in the coordinate plane in the usual way. In which quadrant does it lie?" :question/worked-solution "First, we find an angle that's coterminal with $\\dfrac {8\\pi}3$ by *subtracting* integer multiples of $2\\pi$ until we get a coterminal angle that lies in the range $[0, 2\\pi).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8\\pi }{3}-2\\pi =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the angle $\\dfrac {2\\pi}3$ is coterminal with $\\dfrac {8\\pi}3.$ Let's draw this coterminal angle in the coordinate plane.\n\n![](images/23/2360ed202deeca403c4631c319b69df30a9f0268b7ef270a8e436103c0ac3edb.png)\n\nWe see that $\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}$ lies in quadrant II. Therefore, $\\dfrac{8\\pi}{3}$ also lies in this quadrant."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537888 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059685 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "993c8fe4-dae9-5dc4-9f94-5012f905e0ee" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Correct Quadrant for Negative Angles that Contain More Than One Full Rotation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14499200-08a0-52d8-a84d-ee93f9bba9b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537269} {:answer/id #uuid "8ac9ccee-5559-5bd5-9840-ea449a11fab2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537493} {:answer/id #uuid "b1377c36-6065-59dc-9ecf-a0684bb8d804" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537570} {:answer/id #uuid "c15bb5ff-a135-58be-85f2-e48382a26409" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Between quadrants II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537602} {:answer/id #uuid "c8dab11c-30b4-5a72-b17b-700c26dba4bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537621}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8ac9ccee-5559-5bd5-9840-ea449a11fab2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537493} :answer-field/id #uuid "8ef2b863-b1a2-5dec-97a2-44f43a865e09" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537817}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1327ffff-07aa-5bea-93ff-453e6c9d98a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-124720" :question/problem "The angle $\\theta =-\\frac{11\\pi }{5}$ is represented in the coordinate plane in the usual way. In which quadrant does it lie?" :question/worked-solution "First, we find an angle that's coterminal with $-\\frac{11\\pi }{5}$ by *adding* integer multiples of $2\\pi $ until we get an angle that lies in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$\n\n- $-\\frac{11\\pi }{5}+2\\pi =-\\frac{\\pi }{5}$\n\n- $-\\frac{11\\pi }{5}+2\\cdot (2\\pi )=\\frac{9\\pi }{5}\\,✓$\n\nSo the angle $-\\frac{11\\pi }{5}$ is coterminal with $\\frac{9\\pi }{5}.$ Let's draw this coterminal angle in the coordinate plane.\n\n![](images/4a/4a6bb0dd4991fe1a00bfedc7814f27c4a36b4114fa027673ed9cd03856fcc0d5.png)\n\nWe see that $\\frac{9\\pi }{5}$ lies in quadrant IV. Therefore, $-\\frac{11\\pi }{5}$ also lies in this quadrant."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537945 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059683 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bba0ad98-6940-502a-873c-8a589a03a499" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Coterminal Angles in Degrees"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ef067df-a2f2-5923-854f-168b758fa2b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{212}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537253} {:answer/id #uuid "421f2dac-dcd8-5232-a679-824545657b9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{502}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537366} {:answer/id #uuid "500db5a2-2c49-50cf-aa2a-79d3f0ba6bb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{912}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537396} {:answer/id #uuid "76e052bd-8901-553b-935a-bfdce70be9ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{538}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537466} {:answer/id #uuid "8f4a55b6-48c9-52ea-a3cd-36bfb3dff46e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{158}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537501}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8f4a55b6-48c9-52ea-a3cd-36bfb3dff46e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{158}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537501} :answer-field/id #uuid "fbcaf346-14fa-5491-a96b-990906294b58" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537877}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a22714ba-933f-5202-894c-587dd0012788" :question/math-academy-id "q-124736" :question/problem "Which angle is coterminal with $-{202}^{∘}?$" :question/worked-solution "To find the coterminal angles of a given angle $\\theta $ (in degrees), we add and subtract integer multiples of ${360}^{∘}$\n\nLet's start by adding multiples of ${360}^{∘}$ to $-{202}^{∘}$:\n\n- $-{202}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}={158}^{∘}\\,✓$\n\n- $-{202}^{∘}+2\\cdot {360}^{∘}={518}^{∘}$\n\nThere are no answer choices larger than ${518}^{∘},$ so we can stop here.\n\nNow, let's subtract multiples of ${360}^{∘}$ from $-{202}^{∘}$:\n\n- $-{202}^{∘}-{360}^{∘}=-{562}^{∘}$\n\n- $-{202}^{∘}-2\\cdot {360}^{∘}=-{922}^{∘}$\n\nThere are no answer choices smaller than $-{922}^{∘},$ so we can stop here.\n\nSo the correct answer is ${158}^{∘}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537956 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059684 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2d65819f-0528-527e-8d1a-20421e221ca5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Coterminal Angles in Radians"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c76cf94-8053-5e64-aa82-a82122cb96a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{29\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537287} {:answer/id #uuid "36f02b83-07f0-58d0-bfbb-80ef980ceea1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{23\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537342} {:answer/id #uuid "c9c09866-fa8a-5a31-b2d1-c484025a37d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{17\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537623} {:answer/id #uuid "d6370293-ebf8-5491-947c-6d38067aa92e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537655} {:answer/id #uuid "e422c3e3-c1ab-5384-90f4-4b7d20e5cd4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{12\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537691}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1c76cf94-8053-5e64-aa82-a82122cb96a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{29\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537287} :answer-field/id #uuid "657c9380-e503-5306-a566-c3d47bda5bce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537797}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "af0faf42-b43a-5224-af78-09641aa5c63d" :question/math-academy-id "q-124757" :question/problem "Which angle is coterminal with $\\frac{9\\pi }{5}?$" :question/worked-solution "To find the coterminal angles of a given angle $\\theta $ (in radians), we add and subtract integer multiples of $2\\pi :$\n\nLet's start by adding multiples of $2\\pi $ to $\\frac{9\\pi }{5}$:\n\n- $\\frac{9\\pi }{5}+2\\pi =\\frac{19\\pi }{5}$\n\n- $\\frac{9\\pi }{5}+2\\cdot (2\\pi )=\\frac{29\\pi }{5}\\,✓$\n\nThere are no answer choices larger than $\\frac{29\\pi }{5},$ so we can stop here.\n\nNow, let's subtract multiples of $2\\pi $ from $\\frac{9\\pi }{5}$:\n\n- $\\frac{9\\pi }{5}-2\\pi =-\\frac{\\pi }{5}$\n\n- $\\frac{9\\pi }{5}-2\\cdot (2\\pi )=-\\frac{11\\pi }{5}$\n\n- $\\frac{9\\pi }{5}-3\\cdot (2\\pi )=-\\frac{21\\pi }{5}$\n\nThere are no answer choices smaller than $-\\frac{21\\pi }{5},$ so we can stop here.\n\nSo the correct answer is $\\frac{29\\pi }{5}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537948 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059683 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bba0ad98-6940-502a-873c-8a589a03a499" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Coterminal Angles in Degrees"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03bbb6f4-95c4-59d2-90ba-0d83c32db390" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537233} {:answer/id #uuid "299915e8-7595-5560-8759-70bd258f92d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537312} {:answer/id #uuid "53f5d72f-c59f-552e-87f2-dd36410b9459" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537404} {:answer/id #uuid "d155f444-c7be-5482-8698-ef7013570444" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537637} {:answer/id #uuid "d5847306-0c13-5de4-803c-bbb31889a2f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537649}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "299915e8-7595-5560-8759-70bd258f92d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537312} :answer-field/id #uuid "75cb9fcb-fa4a-5548-b35c-bb9a570cfcd2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537807}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a369ef6f-b58e-5e3b-af34-1de431b7ed56" :question/math-academy-id "q-1679" :question/problem "Which of the following angles are coterminal with $\\theta =-{390}^{∘}?$\n\nI. $-{30}^{∘}$\n\nII. ${305}^{∘}$\n\nIII. $-{750}^{∘}$" :question/worked-solution "To find the coterminal angles of a given angle $\\theta $, we add and subtract integer multiples of ${360}^{∘}:$\n\nLet's start by adding multiples of ${360}^{∘}$ to $-{390}^{∘}$:\n\n- $-{390}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}=-{30}^{∘}\\,✓$\n\n- $-{390}^{∘}+2\\cdot {360}^{∘}={330}^{∘}$\n\nOf the given values, there is no angle greater than ${330}^{∘}.$ So we can stop there.\n\nNow, let's subtract multiples of ${360}^{∘}$ from $-{390}^{∘}$\n\n- $-{390}^{∘}-{360}^{∘}=-{750}^{∘}\\,✓$\n\nOf the given angles, only $-{30}^{∘}$ and $-{750}^{∘}$ are coterminal with $-{390}^{∘}.$\n\nSo the correct answer is I and III only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537952 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059684 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2d65819f-0528-527e-8d1a-20421e221ca5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Coterminal Angles in Radians"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "36162952-7ce9-532c-b904-12a3ca70ccd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537340} {:answer/id #uuid "6929c1e6-6549-5888-b7cb-ab779bbc9769" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537434} {:answer/id #uuid "724de728-6c17-5a4b-a32b-fb8debf6f9fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537453} {:answer/id #uuid "a8891c14-44aa-5ce5-b3eb-d54ddda7534a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537547} {:answer/id #uuid "ad7c0136-e41d-5ea0-9952-8959d50e85b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537557}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "724de728-6c17-5a4b-a32b-fb8debf6f9fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537453} :answer-field/id #uuid "d4bb506e-820b-5bd7-9c88-97a9f7dff0f6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537861}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a6cfd9d2-1fde-532f-8824-00cee2a76d89" :question/math-academy-id "q-22983" :question/problem "Which of the following angles are coterminal with $\\theta =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}?$\n\nI. $\\frac{8\\pi }{3}$\n\nII. $\\frac{14\\pi }{3}$\n\nIII. $-\\frac{10\\pi }{3}$" :question/worked-solution "To find the coterminal angles of a given angle $\\theta $, we add and subtract integer multiples of $2\\pi :$\n\nLet's start by adding multiples of $2\\pi $ to $\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$:\n\n- $\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+2\\pi =\\frac{8\\pi }{3}\\,✓$\n\n- $\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+2\\cdot (2\\pi )=\\frac{14\\pi }{3}\\,✓$\n\nOf the given values, there is no angle greater than $\\frac{14\\pi }{3}.$ So, we can stop there.\n\nNow, let's subtract multiples of $2\\pi $ from $\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$:\n\n- $\\frac{2\\pi }{3}-2\\pi =-\\frac{4\\pi }{3}$\n\n- $\\frac{2\\pi }{3}-2\\cdot (2\\pi )=-\\frac{10\\pi }{3}\\,✓$\n\nOf the given angles, all three are coterminal with $\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.$\n\nSo the correct answer is I, II, and III."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537907 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059686 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0a119150-6d23-59bd-90c9-699de3a75795" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Correct Quadrant for Angles that Contain More Than One Full Rotation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b800297-4e6e-5886-b40a-55cf266d1c00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537387} {:answer/id #uuid "9f1199c3-536c-5b9f-afd9-c28bb4898145" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537527} {:answer/id #uuid "b6751d47-e997-5e08-845d-d41e40b5f80d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Between quadrants II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537582} {:answer/id #uuid "b73363ac-c3cc-52cf-ac01-aa97731a63c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537584} {:answer/id #uuid "c49d0bec-f41b-5b98-8625-bb6185fbaf75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537607}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b73363ac-c3cc-52cf-ac01-aa97731a63c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537584} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a1e73fc-4b88-5be7-88c3-8ba6bc02b059" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537827}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "44b12f59-5bfc-5de8-98fe-816ee27220b4" :question/math-academy-id "q-64674" :question/problem "The angle $\\theta ={1032}^{∘}$ is represented in the coordinate plane in the usual way. In which quadrant does it lie?" :question/worked-solution "First, we find an angle that's coterminal with ${1032}^{∘}$ by *subtracting* integer multiples of ${360}^{∘}$ until we get an angle that lies in the range $[{0}^{∘},{360}^{∘}).$\n\n- ${1032}^{∘}-{360}^{∘}={672}^{∘}$\n\n- ${1032}^{∘}-2\\cdot {360}^{∘}={312}^{∘}\\,✓$\n\nSo the angle ${312}^{∘}$ is coterminal with ${1032}^{∘}.$ Let's draw this coterminal angle in the coordinate plane.\n\n![](images/6d/6db0d45744872518e73e249bc793e48792202c100bf42739fc889febb0fd6c32.png)\n\nWe see that ${312}^{∘}$ lies in quadrant IV. Therefore, ${1032}^{∘}$ also lies in this quadrant."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335733 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059685 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "993c8fe4-dae9-5dc4-9f94-5012f905e0ee" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Correct Quadrant for Negative Angles that Contain More Than One Full Rotation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1fd1a287-023b-55c1-aa72-ee3fee24f836" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333273} {:answer/id #uuid "29f2f53b-f6d3-57e8-8ad8-cd7e46ffff67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333381} {:answer/id #uuid "bd0f0f82-34f4-5ce0-8047-20cf96cbeda3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Between quadrants III and IV" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334632} {:answer/id #uuid "e97a56fa-9091-5cb5-bc68-c8e54a986827" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335032} {:answer/id #uuid "f6fd1610-fe8c-5dc7-9d2a-2ac4b98636fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335155}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "29f2f53b-f6d3-57e8-8ad8-cd7e46ffff67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333381} :answer-field/id #uuid "5593029c-b6f9-5d2e-b2b2-18177c24e6b5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335408}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d5324a1f-6f34-52eb-9030-0465e5ae23da" :question/math-academy-id "q-64675" :question/problem "The angle $\\theta =-{395}^{∘}$ is represented in the coordinate plane in the usual way. In which quadrant does it lie?" :question/worked-solution "First, we find an angle that's coterminal with $-{395}^{∘}$ by *adding* integer multiples of ${360}^{∘}$ until we get an angle that lies in the range $[{0}^{∘},{360}^{∘}).$\n\n- $-{395}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}=-{35}^{∘}$\n\n- $-{395}^{∘}+2\\cdot {360}^{∘}={325}^{∘}\\,✓$\n\nSo the angle ${325}^{∘}$ is coterminal with $-{395}^{∘}.$ Let's draw this coterminal angle in the coordinate plane.\n\n![](images/93/936e222cebb005bc504867f26eb2144bb976cab2441e53051121e056be158be1.png)\n\nWe see that ${325}^{∘}$ lies in quadrant IV. Therefore, $-{395}^{∘}$ also lies in this quadrant."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537927 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059686 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0a119150-6d23-59bd-90c9-699de3a75795" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Correct Quadrant for Angles that Contain More Than One Full Rotation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b4e78cd-5ed8-570d-bf24-7a6c0b064d5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537351} {:answer/id #uuid "3e5318bb-61f3-5058-b0bd-5122c25f1bbd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 1759218604